module.exports = {
  title: 'Routing & Navigation',
  slug: 'routing',
  order: 5,
  topics: [
    {
      title: 'Router Setup, Route & Query Params',
      slug: 'router-params',
      order: 0,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>Angular is a Single Page Application (SPA) framework: the browser loads <code>index.html</code> once and, from then on, the <strong>Router</strong> swaps components in and out of the DOM without a full page reload. Routing is therefore the backbone of any non-trivial Angular app — it maps the URL bar to your component tree, keeps the browser back/forward buttons meaningful, makes every screen deep-linkable and shareable, and gives your app a navigable history without a single server round-trip.</p>`,
        `<p>Reading <strong>route parameters</strong> (the <code>/users/42</code> part of the URL) and <strong>query parameters</strong> (the <code>?sort=name&amp;page=2</code> part) is how a routed component knows <em>what</em> to render. Get this wrong and you produce the two most common routing bugs in existence: "why is my page blank when I refresh?" and "why doesn't my data update when the id in the URL changes but the page stays the same?" Both come from misunderstanding how params flow into a component and when they update. Because these bugs are so universal, interviewers probe params relentlessly — it is the single fastest way to tell whether a candidate has actually shipped an Angular app or only read the tutorial.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>A <strong>route</strong> is a rule: "when the URL path looks like X, render component Y." You register an ordered array of these rules and the Router matches the current URL top-to-bottom, first match wins. Two distinct kinds of dynamic data can travel inside a URL, and mixing them up is a classic mistake.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch6-url-anatomy.svg|Diagram of an Angular URL showing a route param and query params with the paramMap and queryParamMap read APIs|Route params live in the path and identify a resource; query params trail after ? and are optional modifiers.}}`,
        `<table>`,
        `<thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Route (path) param</th><th>Query param</th></tr></thead>`,
        `<tbody>`,
        `<tr><td>Looks like</td><td><code>/users/42</code></td><td><code>/users?id=42</code></td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Defined in route</td><td>Yes — <code>path: 'users/:id'</code></td><td>No — freeform, optional</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Required?</td><td>Part of the match; usually required</td><td>Always optional</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Order matters?</td><td>Yes — positional in the path</td><td>No — order-independent</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Typical use</td><td>Identifying <em>which</em> resource</td><td>Filtering, sorting, pagination, UI state</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Read via</td><td><code>route.paramMap</code></td><td><code>route.queryParamMap</code></td></tr>`,
        `</tbody>`,
        `</table>`,
        `<p>You read both off the injected <code>ActivatedRoute</code> service, and each is available in <strong>two shapes</strong>:</p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>snapshot</strong> — a one-time synchronous read of the params <em>at the moment the component was created</em>. Simple to reach for, but frozen in time.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>observable</strong> (<code>paramMap</code>) — an RxJS stream that emits a fresh value every time the params change, <em>even if the same component instance is being reused</em>.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<p>The distinction is the whole game. Angular reuses a component instance when you navigate from <code>/users/1</code> to <code>/users/2</code> — the route config is the same, so there is no reason to destroy and recreate anything. With <code>snapshot</code> you'd still be looking at user 1; with the observable you react to every change. Choosing between them is the single most tested decision in this topic.</p>`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p>The Router is best understood as a <strong>state machine over the URL</strong>. On every navigation it runs a fixed pipeline: (1) it <em>parses</em> the URL string into a <code>UrlTree</code> — a structured object of path segments, params, and outlets; (2) it walks your <code>Routes</code> array recursively, applying each route's <code>path</code> pattern, to build a matched <code>ActivatedRouteSnapshot</code> tree; (3) it runs guards and resolvers on that candidate tree; and (4) it activates the matched components into their <code>&lt;router-outlet&gt;</code>s. Each activated component is handed an <code>ActivatedRoute</code> service scoped to <em>its</em> node in that tree — which is precisely why the same component class, injected in two positions, sees two different sets of params.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Route matching</strong> is prefix-based and greedy in declaration order. <code>path: 'users/:id'</code> matches any single segment after <code>users/</code> and binds it to <code>id</code>. The empty path <code>path: ''</code> matches when nothing is left to consume — combine it with <code>pathMatch: 'full'</code> for redirects so it doesn't accidentally swallow longer URLs. The wildcard <code>path: '**'</code> matches anything and therefore must be declared <em>last</em>; put it earlier and it shadows every route below it. There is no automatic reordering — the array order <em>is</em> the priority.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>snapshot vs observable</strong> comes down to what value you are holding. <code>paramMap</code> is a <code>BehaviorSubject</code>-backed observable: it replays the current value to any new subscriber, so you never miss the first emission, and it pushes a new value whenever the URL params change. <code>snapshot</code> is literally the latest emitted value captured as a plain object at construction time — it does not update, not because it is broken, but because you are holding a stale reference rather than a live stream. When the URL changes to a route backed by the <em>same component</em>, Angular's default <code>RouteReuseStrategy</code> keeps the instance alive and merely pushes new values through the observables — no new constructor call, no <code>ngOnInit</code>. That reuse is the entire reason snapshot silently breaks on same-route navigation.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch6-snapshot-vs-observable.svg|Comparison of snapshot and paramMap when navigating from users slash 1 to users slash 2 on a reused component|snapshot stays stale because the component is reused; the paramMap observable emits the new id and re-fetches.}}`,
        `<p><code>ParamMap</code> itself is an interface — <code>get(name)</code>, <code>getAll(name)</code>, <code>has(name)</code>, <code>keys</code> — that replaced the older plain <code>params</code> object. <code>getAll</code> exists because a query param can legitimately repeat (<code>?tag=a&amp;tag=b</code>), which a flat object cannot represent. Every value read from a <code>ParamMap</code> is a <strong>string</strong> (or <code>null</code>): URLs have no type system, so <code>+params.get('id')</code> or <code>Number(...)</code> is your job. Finally, the <strong>router lifecycle</strong> is observable end-to-end: <code>router.events</code> streams <code>NavigationStart</code>, <code>RoutesRecognized</code>, <code>NavigationEnd</code>, <code>NavigationCancel</code>, and <code>NavigationError</code>, which you can filter to drive loading bars, analytics, or scroll restoration.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<p>Modern standalone bootstrap wires the router with <code>provideRouter</code> — no <code>RouterModule.forRoot</code>, no NgModule.</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-typescript">// app.routes.ts\nimport { Routes } from '@angular/router';\nimport { UserListComponent } from './user-list.component';\nimport { UserDetailComponent } from './user-detail.component';\n\nexport const routes: Routes = [\n  { path: '', redirectTo: 'users', pathMatch: 'full' },\n  { path: 'users', component: UserListComponent },\n  { path: 'users/:id', component: UserDetailComponent },\n  { path: '**', component: NotFoundComponent }, // wildcard, must be LAST\n];\n\n// main.ts\nimport { bootstrapApplication } from '@angular/platform-browser';\nimport { provideRouter, withComponentInputBinding } from '@angular/router';\nimport { AppComponent } from './app.component';\nimport { routes } from './app.routes';\n\nbootstrapApplication(AppComponent, {\n  providers: [provideRouter(routes, withComponentInputBinding())],\n});</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- Navigating: routerLink builds URLs the Angular way (no full reload) --&gt;\n&lt;a [routerLink]="['/users', user.id]" [queryParams]="{ sort: 'name' }"&gt;\n  {{ user.name }}\n&lt;/a&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- Highlight the active link --&gt;\n&lt;a routerLink="/users" routerLinkActive="active"&gt;All users&lt;/a&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- Where matched components render --&gt;\n&lt;router-outlet&gt;&lt;/router-outlet&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-typescript">// user-detail.component.ts — reactive read (survives id changes)\nimport { Component, inject } from '@angular/core';\nimport { ActivatedRoute } from '@angular/router';\nimport { switchMap, map } from 'rxjs';\n\n@Component({ selector: 'app-user-detail', standalone: true, template: '...' })\nexport class UserDetailComponent {\n  private route = inject(ActivatedRoute);\n  private api = inject(UserService);\n\n  // Re-fetches whenever :id changes, even on the same component instance.\n  // switchMap cancels the in-flight request if the id changes again.\n  user$ = this.route.paramMap.pipe(\n    switchMap(params =&gt; this.api.getUser(params.get('id')!)),\n  );\n\n  // Query params are a separate, independent stream\n  sort$ = this.route.queryParamMap.pipe(map(q =&gt; q.get('sort') ?? 'id'));\n}</code></pre>`,
        `<p>With <code>withComponentInputBinding()</code> (v16+) you can skip <code>ActivatedRoute</code> entirely and bind route params, query params, and resolved data straight to <code>@Input()</code>s. The names must match the param keys:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-typescript">// Route: { path: 'users/:id', component: UserDetailComponent }\nimport { Component, Input } from '@angular/core';\n\n@Component({ /* ... */ })\nexport class UserDetailComponent {\n  @Input() id!: string;        // fed from the :id route param\n  @Input() sort?: string;      // fed from ?sort= query param\n  // In v17.1+ you can use the signal input: id = input.required&lt;string&gt;();\n}</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Using <code>snapshot</code> for a value that can change while the component stays mounted.</strong> Navigating <code>/users/1</code> → <code>/users/2</code> reuses the instance; the snapshot stays on user 1. This is the number-one params bug.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting that params are strings.</strong> <code>params.get('id') === 42</code> is always false; you must convert with <code>Number()</code> or <code>+</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Putting the wildcard <code>'**'</code> route too early.</strong> Routes match top-down, first-win — an early wildcard shadows everything after it and every URL renders the 404.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Omitting <code>pathMatch: 'full'</code> on an empty-path redirect.</strong> A prefix-match empty path matches <em>every</em> URL, so your redirect fires on paths it shouldn't.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Blank page on hard refresh.</strong> The server doesn't know your client-side routes, so refreshing <code>/users/42</code> asks the server for that path and 404s. You need a server rewrite that serves <code>index.html</code> for unknown paths.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Using <code>[href]</code> or a bare <code>&lt;a href&gt;</code> for internal links.</strong> That triggers a full page reload and destroys the SPA. Use <code>routerLink</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Leaking subscriptions</strong> from manually subscribing to <code>paramMap</code> in <code>ngOnInit</code> without unsubscribing. Prefer the <code>async</code> pipe or <code>takeUntilDestroyed()</code>.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Prefer the <code>paramMap</code> observable (or <code>withComponentInputBinding()</code>) over <code>snapshot</code> for anything that can change during the component's lifetime; reserve <code>snapshot</code> for one-shot reads in guards/resolvers or genuinely static routes.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>switchMap</code> when a param change should cancel the previous fetch — it prevents out-of-order responses overwriting the newest one.</li>`,
        `<li>Model "which resource" as a route param and "how to view it" (sort, filter, page) as query params, so the view is bookmarkable and the back button restores UI state.</li>`,
        `<li>Always build internal navigation with <code>routerLink</code> / <code>router.navigate()</code>, never manual anchors, so the Router owns history and change detection.</li>`,
        `<li>Configure a server fallback to <code>index.html</code> (or <code>withHashLocation()</code> for static hosts) so deep links and refreshes work.</li>`,
        `<li>Let the <code>async</code> pipe manage subscriptions to router streams so you never leak.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What's the difference between snapshot and the paramMap observable, and when does snapshot bite you?"</strong><br>A: <code>snapshot</code> is a synchronous one-time read taken at component creation; <code>paramMap</code> is a stream that emits on every param change. Snapshot bites you when you navigate between two URLs that resolve to the <em>same component</em> (e.g. <code>/users/1</code> → <code>/users/2</code>): Angular reuses the instance, <code>ngOnInit</code> doesn't re-run, and your snapshot value is stale. Use the observable — or component input binding — for anything that can change while the component stays mounted.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Route param vs query param — how do you choose?"</strong><br>A: Route params identify <em>which</em> resource and are part of the route's contract (<code>/orders/:id</code>) — the route won't match without them. Query params are optional, order-independent modifiers of the same view (filters, sort, pagination). Rule of thumb: if removing it should 404, it's a route param; if removing it should just show defaults, it's a query param.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why does my page break on hard refresh but work when navigating in-app?"</strong><br>A: Classic SPA trap. In-app navigation is handled by the client Router, but a hard refresh sends the full path to the server, which doesn't know your client routes and 404s. Fix with a server rewrite that serves <code>index.html</code> for unknown paths, or use <code>withHashLocation()</code>.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do you read a param without injecting ActivatedRoute at all?"</strong><br>A: <code>provideRouter(routes, withComponentInputBinding())</code> (v16+) binds route params, query params, and resolved data to matching <code>@Input()</code>s (or signal inputs in v17.1+). It's less boilerplate and makes the component testable as a plain input-driven component.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "A param is numeric but your comparison always fails — why?" (trap)</strong><br>A: <code>ParamMap.get()</code> always returns a string or <code>null</code>. <code>get('id') === 42</code> is false because you're comparing a string to a number. Convert explicitly with <code>Number()</code> / <code>+</code>, and validate it before use.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How would you show a global loading bar during navigation?" (trap on lifecycle)</strong><br>A: Subscribe to <code>router.events</code> and filter for <code>NavigationStart</code> to show it and <code>NavigationEnd</code>/<code>NavigationCancel</code>/<code>NavigationError</code> to hide it. The key insight they're testing is that navigation is an observable lifecycle, not a single synchronous call.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>provideRouter(routes)</code> in bootstrap — no NgModule needed for standalone.</li>`,
        `<li><code>:id</code> in path → route param, read via <code>route.paramMap.get('id')</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><code>?sort=x</code> → query param, read via <code>route.queryParamMap.get('sort')</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>snapshot</strong> = frozen one-time read; <strong>observable</strong> = live, survives same-route navigation.</li>`,
        `<li>Same component reused across <code>/users/1</code>→<code>/users/2</code> ⇒ prefer the observable.</li>`,
        `<li>All param values are strings — convert numbers explicitly.</li>`,
        `<li><code>[routerLink]</code> for in-app nav; <code>routerLinkActive</code> for active styling.</li>`,
        `<li>Wildcard <code>path: '**'</code> goes <strong>last</strong>; routes match top-down, first-win.</li>`,
        `<li>v16+: <code>withComponentInputBinding()</code> binds params directly to <code>@Input()</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><code>router.events</code> streams the navigation lifecycle for loaders/analytics.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },
    {
      title: 'Lazy Loading',
      slug: 'lazy-loading',
      order: 1,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>By default every component you reference from a route ends up in the <strong>initial JavaScript bundle</strong> the browser must download, parse, and execute before your app becomes interactive. On a large app that means users wait on the code for an admin dashboard they may never open, a settings screen behind a login, or a rarely-used report. <strong>Lazy loading</strong> defers loading a route's code until the user actually navigates there, so the first paint ships only a fraction of the bytes.</p>`,
        `<p>This is the highest-leverage performance win in Angular routing. It directly improves Time-to-Interactive (TTI), Largest Contentful Paint, and the Total Blocking Time that Core Web Vitals grade you on — metrics that feed into real business numbers like bounce rate and conversion. It is also a perennial interview favourite precisely because answering it well forces you to reason about bundles, code-splitting, the module dependency graph, and the trade-off between eager and on-demand loading. A candidate who can explain <em>why</em> a dynamic import splits code apart clearly understands how the build actually works.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>Instead of <em>referencing</em> a component directly (which forces the bundler to include it in the main bundle), you hand the Router a <strong>function that dynamically imports it</strong>. The bundler sees the <code>import()</code> call, splits that code into a separate chunk file, and the Router only fetches that chunk when its route is matched at runtime.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch6-lazy-chunks.svg|Comparison of an eager single bundle versus lazy separate chunks loaded on navigation|Eager routes ship in main.js up front; lazy routes are split into chunks fetched only when their route is visited.}}`,
        `<table>`,
        `<thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>Eager (default)</th><th>Lazy</th></tr></thead>`,
        `<tbody>`,
        `<tr><td>Route uses</td><td><code>component: Foo</code></td><td><code>loadComponent: () =&gt; import(...)</code></td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Code ships in</td><td>Main bundle</td><td>Separate chunk, on demand</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Loaded when</td><td>App startup</td><td>First navigation to the route</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Initial bundle size</td><td>Larger</td><td>Smaller</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>DI scope</td><td>Root</td><td>Can carry a feature-scoped injector</td></tr>`,
        `</tbody>`,
        `</table>`,
        `<p>Two APIs, depending on granularity:</p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong><code>loadComponent</code></strong> (v14+) — lazy-load a <em>single</em> standalone component. Perfect for the modern component-per-route style.</li>`,
        `<li><strong><code>loadChildren</code></strong> — lazy-load a whole <em>set of child routes</em> (a feature area). Returns either a routes array (standalone) or, in legacy apps, an NgModule via <code>m.SomeModule</code>.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p>The mechanism is the dynamic <code>import()</code> expression, and understanding it is the whole point. Bundlers — esbuild in the modern Angular CLI, previously Webpack — statically scan your code for each <code>import('...')</code> call. When they find one, they emit that module and its unique dependencies into a <strong>separate chunk file</strong> plus a small runtime shim that knows how to fetch that chunk over the network on demand. Because <code>loadComponent</code> and <code>loadChildren</code> receive a <em>function</em> that <em>returns</em> the import — rather than a direct value — the referenced module is never part of the main entry point's static dependency graph. That is literally, mechanically, why it splits: nothing in the initial bundle imports it eagerly.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Chunk loading at runtime:</strong> when the Router matches a lazy route, it invokes your loader function, which triggers a network request for the chunk. The Router awaits the returned promise — showing nothing (or your loading UI) meanwhile — then activates the resolved component or routes. The chunk is fetched exactly once: the browser HTTP-caches it and the Router keeps its own reference, so a second visit to the route is instant with no new request. If the fetch fails (offline, deploy rotated the hashed filename), the navigation errors — a real production concern worth handling.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Feature-scoped DI:</strong> <code>loadChildren</code> additionally creates a child <strong>environment injector</strong> for the lazily-loaded area. Any <code>providers</code> you attach to a lazy route (or list in its config) are instantiated only when that area loads and live only as long as it is active. That is a genuine memory and startup win beyond bundle size — services for a feature nobody visits are never constructed. <code>loadComponent</code> routes can also carry <code>providers</code> for the same effect at a single-component granularity.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Preloading</strong> hooks into the router lifecycle <em>after</em> the initial render, giving you the best of both worlds: lazy chunks stay out of the initial bundle, yet download quietly in the background before the user clicks. <code>PreloadAllModules</code> walks the whole config and fires every lazy loader once the app is interactive. A custom <code>PreloadingStrategy</code> lets you preload selectively — for example, only routes flagged with <code>data: { preload: true }</code>, so you background-load the three screens users actually reach next and leave the rest truly on-demand.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Where the router shows what during the wait:</strong> while a lazy loader is resolving, the outlet holds the previous content and the navigation lifecycle emits observable events you can hook — <code>NavigationStart</code>, then a <code>RouteConfigLoadStart</code>/<code>RouteConfigLoadEnd</code> pair specifically around the chunk fetch, then <code>NavigationEnd</code>. Filtering <code>router.events</code> for those load events is the clean way to drive a progress indicator that appears only while a chunk is actually downloading, rather than on every in-app navigation. This is also why lazy loading pairs naturally with a good loading skeleton: the one-time fetch is visible and worth designing for, not hiding.</p>`,
        `<p>Finally, note the relationship to the <strong>build output</strong>: each dynamic import becomes a hashed file like <code>chunk-ABC123.js</code> in your <code>dist/</code> folder, listed separately in the <code>ng build</code> stats table under "Lazy Chunk Files". Reading that table is how you verify — empirically, not by hope — that a feature actually split out and that nothing accidentally bloated the initial bundle back up.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-typescript">// app.routes.ts\nimport { Routes } from '@angular/router';\n\nexport const routes: Routes = [\n  { path: '', component: HomeComponent }, // eager: needed immediately\n\n  // Lazy single standalone component\n  {\n    path: 'profile',\n    loadComponent: () =&gt;\n      import('./profile/profile.component').then(m =&gt; m.ProfileComponent),\n  },\n\n  // Lazy feature area — a whole sub-tree of routes with its own providers\n  {\n    path: 'admin',\n    loadChildren: () =&gt; import('./admin/admin.routes').then(m =&gt; m.ADMIN_ROUTES),\n    providers: [AdminApiService], // instantiated only when admin loads\n  },\n];</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-typescript">// admin/admin.routes.ts — the lazily-loaded child routes\nimport { Routes } from '@angular/router';\n\nexport const ADMIN_ROUTES: Routes = [\n  { path: '', loadComponent: () =&gt; import('./admin-home.component').then(m =&gt; m.AdminHomeComponent) },\n  { path: 'users', loadComponent: () =&gt; import('./admin-users.component').then(m =&gt; m.AdminUsersComponent) },\n];</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Smooth over the first-navigation delay with a <strong>preloading strategy</strong> — chunks stay out of the initial bundle but load in the background once the app is interactive:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-typescript">// main.ts\nimport { provideRouter, withPreloading, PreloadAllModules } from '@angular/router';\n\nbootstrapApplication(AppComponent, {\n  providers: [provideRouter(routes, withPreloading(PreloadAllModules))],\n});</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-typescript">// selective-preload.strategy.ts — preload only routes flagged in data\nimport { Injectable } from '@angular/core';\nimport { PreloadingStrategy, Route } from '@angular/router';\nimport { Observable, of } from 'rxjs';\n\n@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })\nexport class SelectivePreload implements PreloadingStrategy {\n  preload(route: Route, load: () =&gt; Observable&lt;unknown&gt;): Observable&lt;unknown&gt; {\n    return route.data?.['preload'] ? load() : of(null);\n  }\n}\n// Route: { path: 'reports', data: { preload: true }, loadComponent: ... }</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Lazy-loading the landing route.</strong> The very first screen the user lands on gains nothing from splitting — you just add a network round-trip before first paint. Keep it eager.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Passing a value instead of a function.</strong> <code>loadComponent: import(...)</code> (calling it immediately) defeats splitting; it must be <code>() =&gt; import(...)</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Importing the lazy component eagerly somewhere else.</strong> If any eager file <code>import</code>s the same component at the top level, it gets pulled back into the main bundle and your split silently disappears.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Not handling chunk-load failure.</strong> A stale hashed filename after a deploy, or an offline user, makes the fetch fail and the navigation error — surface a retry or reload prompt.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Assuming lazy loading fixes runtime speed.</strong> It reduces the <em>initial</em> download; the code still runs when visited. It's a startup optimization, not a general performance cure.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Over-splitting into dozens of tiny chunks.</strong> Too many small requests add HTTP overhead and can hurt more than one right-sized chunk. Split by feature area, not per component blindly.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Keep the initial route(s) eager; lazy-load everything behind a click, a login, or a role.</li>`,
        `<li>Split along feature boundaries with <code>loadChildren</code>, and lazy-load single leaf screens with <code>loadComponent</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Attach feature-scoped <code>providers</code> to lazy routes so services for unvisited areas are never constructed.</li>`,
        `<li>Add a preloading strategy — <code>PreloadAllModules</code> for small apps, a selective strategy for large ones — to hide the first-navigation latency.</li>`,
        `<li>Pair lazy loading with <code>CanMatch</code> guards so unauthorised users never even download a restricted chunk.</li>`,
        `<li>Watch your bundle report (<code>ng build</code> output / source-map-explorer) to confirm chunks actually split and nothing leaked back into main.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How does lazy loading actually reduce the initial bundle — what's the mechanism?"</strong><br>A: You pass the Router a function returning a dynamic <code>import()</code> instead of a direct component/module reference. The bundler code-splits each dynamic import into its own chunk, excluding that code from the main entry bundle. The chunk is downloaded only when the user navigates to the route, cutting the JS the browser parses on first load and improving Time-to-Interactive.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "loadComponent vs loadChildren — when do you reach for each?"</strong><br>A: <code>loadComponent</code> lazy-loads one standalone component — ideal for a single leaf route. <code>loadChildren</code> lazy-loads a whole feature area (a routes array or a legacy NgModule) and can carry its own <code>providers</code> in a feature-scoped injector. Use <code>loadChildren</code> when the area has multiple routes and/or its own services; <code>loadComponent</code> for a single screen.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Doesn't lazy loading just move the delay to navigation time? How do you avoid a janky first click?"</strong><br>A: Yes, there's a one-time fetch on first visit. Mitigate with a preloading strategy — <code>PreloadAllModules</code> or a custom one that preloads high-probability routes after the app is interactive — so chunks download in the background while staying out of the initial bundle.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "You added loadComponent but the bundle didn't shrink — why?" (trap)</strong><br>A: Almost always because the component is still imported eagerly somewhere — another eager module has a top-level <code>import</code> of it, pulling it back into the main graph. The dynamic import only splits code that nothing else eagerly references. Check the bundle analyzer.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do you make sure non-admins never even download the admin code?"</strong><br>A: Combine lazy <code>loadChildren</code> with a <code>CanMatch</code> guard. <code>CanMatch</code> runs during route matching and, if it returns false, the route is skipped and its chunk is never fetched — so the admin JavaScript never reaches an unauthorised browser. <code>CanActivate</code> would run too late, after the chunk could already load.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What happens if a lazy chunk fails to load in production?"</strong><br>A: The navigation emits a <code>NavigationError</code>. This commonly happens after a redeploy rotates hashed filenames while a user has the old <code>index.html</code>. Handle it by listening on <code>router.events</code> and prompting a reload, or by using deployment strategies that keep old chunks available.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>loadComponent: () =&gt; import('...').then(m =&gt; m.Cmp)</code> — lazy one standalone component (v14+).</li>`,
        `<li><code>loadChildren: () =&gt; import('...').then(m =&gt; m.ROUTES)</code> — lazy a feature's routes.</li>`,
        `<li>Splitting works because you pass a <strong>function</strong> with a dynamic <code>import()</code>, keeping the code out of the main graph.</li>`,
        `<li>Cuts initial bundle → faster first paint / Time-to-Interactive.</li>`,
        `<li><code>withPreloading(PreloadAllModules)</code> to background-load chunks after startup.</li>`,
        `<li>Attach feature-scoped <code>providers</code> to a lazy route for on-demand DI.</li>`,
        `<li>Gate lazy chunks with <code>CanMatch</code> so restricted code never downloads.</li>`,
        `<li>Don't lazy-load the very first route the user lands on.</li>`,
        `<li>If the bundle didn't shrink, something still imports the component eagerly.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },
    {
      title: 'Route Guards',
      slug: 'route-guards',
      order: 2,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>Guards are the Router's checkpoints: functions that run <em>before</em> a navigation completes and decide whether it may proceed, be redirected, or be cancelled. They enforce authentication ("must be logged in"), authorization ("must be an admin"), and UX safety ("you have unsaved changes — leave anyway?"). <strong>Resolvers</strong> are the sibling concept: they pre-fetch data before the component activates so the screen never flashes empty and then pops in.</p>`,
        `<p>Guards are security- and correctness-critical, so interviewers use them to test two things at once. First, whether you understand that navigation is <em>asynchronous and cancellable</em> — a guard can return a Promise or Observable, and a newer navigation can supersede one in flight. Second, whether you've kept up with Angular's shift from class-based guards to <strong>functional guards</strong> (introduced v14.2, class-based deprecated in v15.2). A candidate still writing <code>@Injectable</code> guard classes with <code>implements CanActivate</code> is signalling they stopped learning around 2021.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>Each guard is a function attached to a route that returns <code>true</code> (allow), <code>false</code> (block), or a <code>UrlTree</code> (cancel and redirect) — synchronously, or asynchronously as a <code>Promise</code> or <code>Observable</code>. There are four guard hooks plus resolvers, each answering a different question:</p>`,
        `<table>`,
        `<thead><tr><th>Guard</th><th>Runs when</th><th>Answers</th></tr></thead>`,
        `<tbody>`,
        `<tr><td><code>CanActivate</code></td><td>Before entering a route</td><td>"May the user open this page?"</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>CanActivateChild</code></td><td>Before entering any child route</td><td>"May they open a child of this?"</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>CanDeactivate</code></td><td>Before leaving a route</td><td>"May they navigate away?" (unsaved changes)</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>CanMatch</code></td><td>While <em>matching</em> the route</td><td>"Should this route even be considered?"</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>Resolve</code></td><td>After guards pass, before activation</td><td>"Fetch this data first"</td></tr>`,
        `</tbody>`,
        `</table>`,
        `<p><strong><code>CanMatch</code> is the sharp one.</strong> Unlike <code>CanActivate</code> — which runs after a route is matched and only blocks activation — <code>CanMatch</code> decides whether the route <em>matches at all</em>. If it returns false, the Router skips this route and tries the next one. That enables two routes with the same path resolving to different components by role, and, crucially, it runs <em>before</em> a lazy chunk is downloaded, so you can block loading admin code for non-admins entirely.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch6-canmatch-vs-canactivate.svg|Side by side of CanActivate and CanMatch on a lazy route showing CanActivate may download the chunk while CanMatch prevents it|CanActivate blocks activation but the lazy chunk may already download; CanMatch skips the route during matching so the chunk is never fetched.}}`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p>Guards run during the Router's <strong>navigation pipeline</strong> — a chain of RxJS operators over the pending <code>UrlTree</code>. The order is deliberate and worth memorising because interviewers ask for it exactly: <strong>CanMatch</strong> runs first, during route matching, so it can veto lazy loading; then, for the resolved path, the Router runs <strong>CanDeactivate</strong> on the outgoing route(s) you're leaving; then <strong>CanActivate</strong> and <strong>CanActivateChild</strong> on the incoming route(s) you're entering; and finally the <strong>resolvers</strong> for the target route. Only after all of that does the component activate and render.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch6-guard-pipeline.svg|Left to right pipeline of CanMatch then CanDeactivate then CanActivate then Resolve then Activate|Guards run in a fixed order; any false cancels the navigation and a returned UrlTree redirects.}}`,
        `<p><strong>Combination semantics:</strong> guards at the same level run in parallel and their results are combined — the navigation proceeds only if <em>every</em> guard resolves to <code>true</code>. The first guard that yields <code>false</code> cancels the navigation (emitting <code>NavigationCancel</code>). A returned <code>UrlTree</code> is special: it cancels the current navigation and atomically starts a fresh navigation to that tree — which is why returning <code>router.createUrlTree(['/login'])</code> is preferred over calling <code>router.navigate()</code> and returning <code>false</code> (the latter is two steps and can race).</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Why async and cancellable matters:</strong> because a guard may return an <code>Observable</code> or <code>Promise</code>, the whole navigation is asynchronous. If the user triggers a new navigation while a guard is still resolving, the Router can abandon the in-flight one — so guards must be idempotent and side-effect-light.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Why functional guards work:</strong> the Router executes each functional guard inside an <strong>injection context</strong> it sets up for the pending navigation, which is exactly what makes <code>inject()</code> legal inside a plain function. That is the entire reason class guards became unnecessary — you no longer need an <code>@Injectable</code> class just to get dependencies; you call <code>inject(AuthService)</code> directly.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>The resolver gotcha:</strong> by default the Router <em>waits</em> for every resolver to complete before showing the component, and the previous page stays on screen meanwhile. A slow resolver stalls navigation; a resolver whose observable <em>never completes</em> (e.g. a hot stream, or forgetting <code>first()</code>) freezes navigation forever with no error and no UI feedback. Always ensure the stream completes and handle its errors, or the app appears hung.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>How components read guard/resolver output:</strong> resolved data lands on <code>route.data</code> under the key you gave it in the <code>resolve: { user: userResolver }</code> map, readable via <code>route.data</code> (an observable) or — with <code>withComponentInputBinding()</code> — bound straight to a matching <code>@Input() user</code>. Guards, by contrast, produce no data; they only gate. Keeping that split clear matters in interviews: guards answer "may I?", resolvers answer "with what?". Conflating them — for example, doing a permission check inside a resolver — leads to awkward flows where the page half-loads before you discover the user wasn't allowed in.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Guards and the injection context lifetime:</strong> the injection context the Router sets up exists only for the duration of that guard call. That means you can <code>inject()</code> at the top of the function body but not inside an async callback that runs later — by then the context is gone and <code>inject()</code> throws. Capture your dependencies into <code>const</code>s at the start of the guard, then use them freely inside <code>pipe()</code> operators or <code>then()</code> callbacks. This single rule prevents the most common "inject() must be called from an injection context" runtime error in guard code.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<p>Modern functional guards are just injectable functions — no class, no <code>implements</code>. Use <code>inject()</code> for dependencies.</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-typescript">// guards/auth.guard.ts\nimport { inject } from '@angular/core';\nimport { CanActivateFn, CanMatchFn, Router } from '@angular/router';\nimport { AuthService } from '../auth.service';\n\nexport const authGuard: CanActivateFn = (route, state) =&gt; {\n  const auth = inject(AuthService);\n  const router = inject(Router);\n  if (auth.isLoggedIn()) return true;\n  // Return a UrlTree to redirect (atomic — preferred over manual navigate)\n  return router.createUrlTree(['/login'], { queryParams: { returnUrl: state.url } });\n};\n\n// CanMatch: blocks the route from matching AND blocks the lazy chunk download\nexport const adminMatch: CanMatchFn = () =&gt; {\n  return inject(AuthService).hasRole('admin');\n};</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-typescript">// guards/unsaved-changes.guard.ts — CanDeactivate\nimport { CanDeactivateFn } from '@angular/router';\n\nexport interface HasUnsaved { hasUnsavedChanges(): boolean; }\n\nexport const unsavedGuard: CanDeactivateFn&lt;HasUnsaved&gt; = (component) =&gt; {\n  return component.hasUnsavedChanges()\n    ? confirm('Discard unsaved changes?')\n    : true;\n};</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-typescript">// guards/user.resolver.ts — functional resolver (ResolveFn)\nimport { inject } from '@angular/core';\nimport { ResolveFn } from '@angular/router';\nimport { catchError, of } from 'rxjs';\n\nexport const userResolver: ResolveFn&lt;User | null&gt; = (route) =&gt; {\n  return inject(UserService)\n    .getUser(route.paramMap.get('id')!)     // HTTP completes, so it's safe\n    .pipe(catchError(() =&gt; of(null)));       // never let it hang on error\n};</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-typescript">// app.routes.ts — wiring them up\nexport const routes: Routes = [\n  { path: 'login', component: LoginComponent },\n  {\n    path: 'account',\n    canActivate: [authGuard],\n    canDeactivate: [unsavedGuard],\n    component: AccountComponent,\n    resolve: { user: userResolver }, // component reads it via route.data or an @Input\n  },\n  {\n    path: 'admin',\n    canMatch: [adminMatch], // non-admins never download this chunk\n    loadChildren: () =&gt; import('./admin/admin.routes').then(m =&gt; m.ADMIN_ROUTES),\n  },\n];</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Using <code>CanActivate</code> to gate a lazy admin area.</strong> It blocks activation but runs after matching, so the chunk can already download. Use <code>CanMatch</code> to prevent the download entirely.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Calling <code>router.navigate()</code> and returning <code>false</code> to redirect.</strong> That's two operations and can race; return a <code>UrlTree</code> instead for an atomic redirect.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>A resolver whose observable never completes.</strong> The Router waits forever and the app looks frozen. Ensure completion (HTTP completes; add <code>first()</code> to hot streams) and handle errors.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Heavy resolvers hurting perceived speed.</strong> The old page stays visible while the resolver runs, so a slow one feels like a hang. Many teams prefer loading in-component with a spinner instead.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Writing class-based guards in new code.</strong> Deprecated since v15.2; functional guards are the standard and far less boilerplate.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Putting side effects in guards.</strong> Navigation can be cancelled or superseded, so a guard that mutates state or fires analytics may do so for a navigation that never lands.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting error handling.</strong> An unhandled error thrown in a guard/resolver emits <code>NavigationError</code> and can leave the user stuck on the old page silently.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Write functional guards (<code>CanActivateFn</code>, <code>CanMatchFn</code>, <code>CanDeactivateFn</code>, <code>ResolveFn</code>) using <code>inject()</code>; retire class-based ones.</li>`,
        `<li>Redirect by returning <code>router.createUrlTree([...])</code> — atomic and race-free — rather than imperative <code>navigate()</code> + <code>false</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>CanMatch</code> for role/feature gating of lazy areas so restricted code never downloads; use <code>CanActivate</code> for simpler per-page auth on eager routes.</li>`,
        `<li>Keep guards pure and fast; move real work into services and keep side effects out.</li>`,
        `<li>For resolvers, guarantee the stream completes and always <code>catchError</code>; reserve resolvers for small, fast, must-have data and use in-component loading for the rest.</li>`,
        `<li>Preserve intended destination on auth redirects with a <code>returnUrl</code> query param so login can bounce the user back.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "CanActivate vs CanMatch — why would you pick CanMatch for an admin area?"</strong><br>A: <code>CanActivate</code> runs after the route is matched and only blocks <em>activation</em> — with a lazy route, the chunk may already download. <code>CanMatch</code> runs during matching: if it returns false the route is skipped entirely and, for a lazy route, the chunk is never fetched. So for role-gating a lazy admin area, <code>CanMatch</code> both hides the route and avoids shipping admin code to unauthorised users. Bonus: two routes with the same path can resolve to different components using <code>CanMatch</code>.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Show me the modern way to write a guard and redirect."</strong><br>A: A functional guard — a <code>CanActivateFn</code> that uses <code>inject()</code> for dependencies and returns <code>true</code>, <code>false</code>, or a <code>UrlTree</code>. Prefer returning <code>router.createUrlTree([...])</code> for redirects rather than calling <code>router.navigate()</code> and returning false; the <code>UrlTree</code> approach is atomic — it cancels the current navigation and triggers the redirect in one step, avoiding a race. Class-based guards are deprecated since v15.2.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What's the danger with resolvers?"</strong><br>A: The Router blocks navigation until the resolver completes, so a resolver whose observable never completes — or errors unhandled — freezes navigation with no UI feedback. Keep them fast, make sure the stream completes (HTTP does; add <code>first()</code> to hot streams), and always <code>catchError</code>. Heavy resolvers also hurt perceived performance because the previous page stays visible while you wait.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "In what exact order do guards and resolvers run?"</strong><br>A: <code>CanMatch</code> (during matching) → <code>CanDeactivate</code> (leaving the old route) → <code>CanActivate</code> / <code>CanActivateChild</code> (entering the new route) → <code>Resolve</code> → component activation. Same-level guards run in parallel and all must pass; the first <code>false</code> cancels, a <code>UrlTree</code> redirects.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How does inject() work inside a plain guard function?"</strong><br>A: The Router runs functional guards inside an injection context it establishes for the pending navigation, so <code>inject()</code> resolves against the correct injector. That's precisely why functional guards replaced class guards — you no longer need an <code>@Injectable</code> class just to obtain dependencies.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "You block a route with CanActivate returning false — user clicks fast and something weird happens. Why?" (trap)</strong><br>A: Because navigation is async and cancellable, and imperative redirects inside guards can race with the cancelled navigation. Returning a <code>UrlTree</code> makes cancel-and-redirect a single atomic operation; mixing <code>navigate()</code> with <code>return false</code> creates two competing navigations.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Functional guards (v14.2+): plain functions using <code>inject()</code>; class guards deprecated (v15.2).</li>`,
        `<li>Return <code>true</code> / <code>false</code> / <code>UrlTree</code>; may be sync or <code>Promise</code>/<code>Observable</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><code>CanActivate</code> = entering; <code>CanDeactivate</code> = leaving (unsaved-changes); <code>CanActivateChild</code> = children.</li>`,
        `<li><code>CanMatch</code> = decides if the route matches at all — runs <strong>before</strong> lazy-chunk download.</li>`,
        `<li>Redirect via <code>router.createUrlTree([...])</code> (atomic) rather than <code>navigate()</code> + return false.</li>`,
        `<li>Resolvers pre-fetch data; Router waits for completion — ensure the stream completes and <code>catchError</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Order: CanMatch → CanDeactivate → CanActivate(Child) → Resolve.</li>`,
        `<li>Keep guards pure and side-effect-free; navigation may be cancelled.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },
    {
      title: 'Child Routes & Named Outlets',
      slug: 'child-routes',
      order: 3,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>Real apps aren't flat lists of pages — they nest. A settings page has a persistent sidebar plus swappable sub-panels; a dashboard has a fixed shell with an inner content area; a mail client shows a list and a preview at the same time. <strong>Child routes</strong> let a parent component stay mounted while its <code>&lt;router-outlet&gt;</code> swaps children, mirroring your URL hierarchy (<code>/settings/profile</code>, <code>/settings/billing</code>). Because the parent isn't destroyed, its state, scroll position, and subscriptions survive as the user moves between sub-screens.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Named (secondary) outlets</strong> go further: they let you render more than one routed region at once — a detail panel, a chat sidebar, a routable modal — each controlled independently by the URL and each deep-linkable and back-button-friendly. Interviewers ask about both to see whether you understand the Router as a <em>tree of outlets</em> rather than a single slot, and whether you can read and produce the unusual auxiliary-route URL syntax that trips up almost everyone the first time they meet it.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>Every <code>&lt;router-outlet&gt;</code> is a slot. Routes have a <code>children</code> array whose components render into the outlet declared in the <em>parent's</em> template. Nesting routes ⇒ nesting outlets ⇒ a tree.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch6-outlet-tree.svg|Tree diagram showing an app shell outlet with a child inbox parent that has a primary list outlet and a named preview outlet in parallel|The Router builds a tree of outlets: child routes nest into the parent's outlet, while a named outlet renders in parallel.}}`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Child routes</strong> — <code>{ path: 'settings', children: [...] }</code>. The parent (<code>SettingsComponent</code>) has its own <code>&lt;router-outlet&gt;</code>; children fill it. The parent persists across child navigation.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Named outlet</strong> — <code>&lt;router-outlet name="detail"&gt;</code>. A route targets it with <code>outlet: 'detail'</code>. Multiple outlets can render simultaneously.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<table>`,
        `<thead><tr><th></th><th>Primary / child outlet</th><th>Named (secondary) outlet</th></tr></thead>`,
        `<tbody>`,
        `<tr><td>Template</td><td><code>&lt;router-outlet&gt;</code></td><td><code>&lt;router-outlet name="x"&gt;</code></td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Route config</td><td><code>children: [...]</code></td><td><code>{ ..., outlet: 'x' }</code></td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>URL shape</td><td><code>/settings/profile</code></td><td><code>/list(x:detail/42)</code></td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>How many at once</td><td>One per outlet, nested</td><td>Several in parallel</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Link syntax</td><td><code>routerLink="profile"</code></td><td><code>[{ outlets: { x: [...] } }]</code></td></tr>`,
        `</tbody>`,
        `</table>`,
        `<p>That parenthesised <code>(x:detail/42)</code> is Angular's <strong>auxiliary route</strong> syntax: the primary URL first, then each named outlet's segment inside parentheses as <code>outletName:path</code>.</p>`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p>The Router does not compute a single "active route" — it builds an <strong>ActivatedRoute tree</strong> that mirrors your nested config, and each <code>&lt;router-outlet&gt;</code> subscribes to its corresponding node in that tree. When you navigate within a nested area, the Router diffs the new tree against the old one and only re-activates the <em>subtree under the node that changed</em>. That is exactly why the parent shell — its component instance, local state, scroll position, and subscriptions — survives when you switch from <code>/settings/profile</code> to <code>/settings/billing</code>: the parent node is unchanged, so only the child outlet's occupant is destroyed and its replacement created.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Route matching for children</strong> is recursive and consumes the URL segment by segment. Matching <code>settings</code> consumes that segment and hands the remainder (<code>profile</code>) to the <code>children</code> array, which matches it against its own routes. An empty-path child <code>{ path: '', redirectTo: 'profile', pathMatch: 'full' }</code> gives a default sub-route when the parent path is hit bare. <strong>Relative links</strong> matter here: <code>routerLink="profile"</code> without a leading slash resolves against the current <code>ActivatedRoute</code> node, so it Just Works inside a child template without repeating the parent path — whereas a leading <code>/</code> resets to the root.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Named outlets</strong> exist because the Router models active state as a tree in which <em>siblings can be active in parallel</em>: the primary outlet and any secondary outlets at the same level are independent branches. The parenthesised URL segment — <code>(outletName:segment)</code> — is Angular's serialization of those parallel branches, which is why auxiliary URLs look unusual and why you address them with the <code>{ outlets: { name: [...] } }</code> link object rather than a flat path array. Setting an outlet to <code>null</code> in that object removes its branch from the <code>UrlTree</code>, cleanly de-activating just that outlet while leaving the primary content untouched. Each secondary outlet has its own <code>ActivatedRoute</code>, its own params, and its own lifecycle, fully independent of the primary.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Change detection and reuse across children:</strong> because the parent node isn't re-created on child navigation, its <code>ngOnInit</code> runs exactly once for the lifetime of the shell — a fact people forget when they put "load the sidebar data" logic in the child instead of the parent, or vice versa. If you genuinely need to react to <em>which</em> child is active from inside the parent, subscribe to the parent's <code>ActivatedRoute.firstChild</code> or to <code>router.events</code>, since no new parent construction will notify you. Conversely, per-child setup belongs in the child's own <code>ngOnInit</code>, which does run each time that child activates. Getting this placement right is the difference between a shell that fetches its nav data once and one that hammers the server on every tab click.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Guards, resolvers, and data on children:</strong> child routes carry their own guards and resolvers, and <code>CanActivateChild</code> on the parent is a convenient single place to gate <em>every</em> child at once — handy for an authenticated area where each sub-page shares the same "must be logged in" rule. Resolved data and params also compose down the tree: a child can read its parent's params via <code>route.parent.paramMap</code>, which is how a nested <code>/orders/:orderId/items/:itemId</code> structure lets the items view see the order id without re-parsing the URL.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-typescript">// app.routes.ts — nested children + a named outlet\nexport const routes: Routes = [\n  {\n    path: 'settings',\n    component: SettingsComponent, // the shell — has &lt;router-outlet&gt;\n    children: [\n      { path: '', redirectTo: 'profile', pathMatch: 'full' },\n      { path: 'profile', component: ProfileComponent },\n      { path: 'billing', component: BillingComponent },\n    ],\n  },\n  {\n    path: 'inbox',\n    component: InboxComponent, // has BOTH a primary and a named outlet\n    children: [\n      { path: '', component: MessageListComponent },\n      // Renders into &lt;router-outlet name="preview"&gt;\n      { path: 'message/:id', component: MessagePreviewComponent, outlet: 'preview' },\n    ],\n  },\n];</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- settings.component.html: children render here, parent persists --&gt;\n&lt;nav&gt;\n  &lt;a routerLink="profile" routerLinkActive="active"&gt;Profile&lt;/a&gt;\n  &lt;a routerLink="billing" routerLinkActive="active"&gt;Billing&lt;/a&gt;\n&lt;/nav&gt;\n&lt;router-outlet&gt;&lt;/router-outlet&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- inbox.component.html: two outlets side by side --&gt;\n&lt;router-outlet&gt;&lt;/router-outlet&gt;                 &lt;!-- primary: the list --&gt;\n&lt;aside&gt;\n  &lt;router-outlet name="preview"&gt;&lt;/router-outlet&gt; &lt;!-- secondary: the preview --&gt;\n&lt;/aside&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- Open a preview in the named outlet WITHOUT leaving the list --&gt;\n&lt;a [routerLink]="[{ outlets: { preview: ['message', msg.id] } }]"&gt;\n  {{ msg.subject }}\n&lt;/a&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- Close the named outlet: set it to null --&gt;\n&lt;a [routerLink]="[{ outlets: { preview: null } }]"&gt;Close&lt;/a&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>The resulting URL for an open preview looks like <code>/inbox(preview:message/42)</code> — the list stays put while the aside swaps.</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-typescript">// Navigating a named outlet imperatively from code\nimport { inject } from '@angular/core';\nimport { Router } from '@angular/router';\n\nexport class InboxComponent {\n  private router = inject(Router);\n  open(id: string) {\n    this.router.navigate([{ outlets: { preview: ['message', id] } }]);\n  }\n  close() {\n    this.router.navigate([{ outlets: { preview: null } }]);\n  }\n}</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting <code>outlet: 'name'</code> in the route config.</strong> The component silently never appears — the route matches the primary outlet, which has nowhere to render a secondary.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Missing the <code>&lt;router-outlet&gt;</code> in the parent template.</strong> Child routes have no slot to render into, so navigating produces a blank inner area.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Omitting <code>pathMatch: 'full'</code> on an empty-path child redirect.</strong> Prefix matching makes it match too eagerly and hijack sibling routes.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Using an absolute path for a child link.</strong> <code>routerLink="/profile"</code> resets to root; you usually want the relative <code>routerLink="profile"</code> so it resolves under the parent.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Trying to address a named outlet with a flat path array.</strong> Secondary outlets need the <code>[{ outlets: { name: [...] } }]</code> object form, not <code>['message', id]</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting how to close a secondary outlet.</strong> You must set it to <code>null</code>; navigating the primary alone leaves the aux segment in the URL.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Over-nesting.</strong> Deeply nested outlets make URLs and mental models hard; flatten where the parent adds no persistent shell.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Use child routes whenever there's a persistent shell (sidebar, tabs, header) that should survive inner navigation — let the parent hold that state.</li>`,
        `<li>Give every parent with <code>children</code> a default via an empty-path <code>redirectTo</code> + <code>pathMatch: 'full'</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Prefer relative <code>routerLink</code>s inside child templates so refactoring the parent path doesn't break links.</li>`,
        `<li>Reach for named outlets when a secondary region (preview, chat, modal) must be independently deep-linkable and controlled by the URL — not for ordinary content that belongs in the primary flow.</li>`,
        `<li>Address secondary outlets with the <code>{ outlets: {...} }</code> object form, and close them by setting the outlet to <code>null</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Keep outlet nesting shallow and intentional; every extra outlet is extra URL complexity for whoever debugs it later.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do nested/child routes keep the parent component alive?"</strong><br>A: Child routes activate into a <code>&lt;router-outlet&gt;</code> declared in the parent's template. The Router builds an ActivatedRoute tree, and navigating between siblings only re-activates the child subtree — the parent node (and its component instance, state, subscriptions) is untouched. That's how you get persistent shells: a sidebar/header that stays put while the inner content swaps.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What's a named/secondary outlet and when would you use one?"</strong><br>A: A <code>&lt;router-outlet name="x"&gt;</code> is an additional routed slot that renders in parallel with the primary outlet, driven independently by the URL. Use it for a detail preview, a chat panel, or a routable modal you want deep-linkable and back-button-friendly without navigating away from the main content. You target it with <code>outlet: 'x'</code> in the route and the <code>{ outlets: { x: [...] } }</code> link syntax; the URL serializes as <code>/main(x:sub)</code>.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why does my named-outlet routerLink look so weird, and how do I close the outlet?"</strong><br>A: Secondary outlets aren't addressed by a normal path — you pass a link object <code>[{ outlets: { preview: ['message', id] } }]</code>, which produces the parenthesised auxiliary URL. To close it, set that outlet to <code>null</code>: <code>[{ outlets: { preview: null } }]</code>.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "My secondary component won't render even though the route looks right — why?" (trap)</strong><br>A: Almost always a missing <code>outlet: 'name'</code> on the route or a missing <code>&lt;router-outlet name="name"&gt;</code> in the template. Without the matching <code>outlet</code> key, the route resolves against the primary outlet and there's nothing to show it.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Relative vs absolute routerLink inside a child template — what's the difference?"</strong><br>A: A leading slash (<code>/profile</code>) resolves from the app root; no leading slash (<code>profile</code>) resolves relative to the current <code>ActivatedRoute</code> node. Inside a child template you almost always want relative, so links keep working if the parent path changes.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How would you make a modal that's shareable and survives the back button?"</strong><br>A: Model it as a named outlet. Opening the modal navigates <code>[{ outlets: { modal: [...] } }]</code>, producing a URL like <code>/page(modal:confirm)</code> that's deep-linkable; closing sets the outlet to <code>null</code>; and because it's a real navigation, the back button dismisses it naturally.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Child routes = <code>children: [...]</code> rendering into the parent's <code>&lt;router-outlet&gt;</code>; parent persists.</li>`,
        `<li>Empty-path child <code>{ path: '', redirectTo: 'x', pathMatch: 'full' }</code> for a default sub-route.</li>`,
        `<li>Named outlet: template <code>&lt;router-outlet name="x"&gt;</code> + route <code>outlet: 'x'</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Multiple named outlets render in parallel with the primary one.</li>`,
        `<li>Auxiliary URL shape: <code>/main(x:sub/42)</code>; link via <code>[{ outlets: { x: [...] } }]</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Close a named outlet by setting it to <code>null</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Relative <code>routerLink</code> (no leading <code>/</code>) resolves against the current route node.</li>`,
        `<li>Missing <code>outlet:</code> or a missing named <code>&lt;router-outlet&gt;</code> ⇒ component silently doesn't render.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },
  ],
};
